uare-integrable yield R, where the maximal Sharpe ratio available by trading is finite but the maximal monotone Sharpe ratio is infinite Example 4.6 exhibits a complete market model with an equivalent martingale measure; where both the MV utility and the MMV utility are infinite.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":407.8900146484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.0,"r":525.3333333333334,"t":550.8900146484375},"charspan":[0,856],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"Example 4.1 illustrates that the locally optimal strategy may not be unique even if there is no redundancy among the traded assets. Example 4.2 contrasts classical mean-variance and monotone mean variance optimal portfolios in a continuous-time setting: Example 4.3 shows that the equivalences in Theorem 2.25 fail when R is not square integrable. Example 4.4 illustrates that can be equivalent   to P without being a ơ-martingale measure for R, again highlighting the importance of the square-integrability   assumption in Theorem 2.25. Example 4.5 constructs a model with square-integrable yield R, where the maximal Sharpe ratio available by trading is finite but the maximal monotone Sharpe ratio is infinite Example 4.6 exhibits a complete market model with an equivalent martingale measure; where both the MV utility and the MMV utility are infinite."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Example 4.1. Consider a one-period model with four atoms and two assets, whose yield distribution is given by","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":370.2233479817708,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.0,"r":525.0,"t":398.2233479817708},"charspan":[0,109],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Example 4.1. Consider a one-period model with four atoms and two assets, whose yield distribution is given by"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Choosing 9 (0.62,0.18,0.1,0.1) yields an equivalent martingale measure for R; hence the model is arbitrage-free. Observe that in this   model, all   separating measures are martingale measures for R. One easily verifies that ậ = (0.5,0.5,0,0) defines 0 martingale measure whose density satisfies","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":244.22334798177087,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.66666666666667,"r":525.6666666666666,"t":302.55668131510413},"charspan":[0,295],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"Choosing 9 (0.62,0.18,0.1,0.1) yields an equivalent martingale measure for R; hence the model is arbitrage-free. Observe that in this   model, all   separating measures are martingale measures for R. One easily verifies that ậ = (0.5,0.5,0,0) defines 0 martingale measure whose density satisfies"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"2 Var dP 3","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":214.22334798177087,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":260.3333333333333,"r":332.0,"t":238.8900146484375},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The duality constraint in Proposition 2.20 and (2.7) yield that twice the maximal utility cannot exceed 1 = 0.4. On the other hand, one has E[2gMMv ( RI )] E[2gMmv ( R2)] = 0.4 hence we have two distinct optimal strategies; each investing one dollar in one of the assets, respectively; and borrowing one dollar at the zero risk-free rate. affine combination of these two strategies is optimal. The wealth of all optimal strategies coincides below the bliss of the utility function. Any again point","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":121.55668131510413,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.46836518478074,"r":526.0,"t":209.22334798177087},"charspan":[0,497],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"The duality constraint in Proposition 2.20 and (2.7) yield that twice the maximal utility cannot exceed 1 = 0.4. On the other hand, one has E[2gMMv ( RI )] E[2gMmv ( R2)] = 0.4 hence we have two distinct optimal strategies; each investing one dollar in one of the assets, respectively; and borrowing one dollar at the zero risk-free rate. affine combination of these two strategies is optimal. The wealth of all optimal strategies coincides below the bliss of the utility function. Any again point"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In this example; 2/3 is the maximal squared monotone Sharpe ratio among zero-cost strategies Hansen ratio among zero-cost strategies.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":79.55668131510413,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.66666666666667,"r":525.6666666666666,"t":122.55668131510413},"charspan":[0,133],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/13","text":"In this example; 2/3 is the maximal squared monotone Sharpe ratio among zero-cost strategies Hansen ratio among zero-cost strategies."}],"version":"1.10.0"}